{"name":"Canoe Blue","hex":"#1d5671","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"canoe","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small long and narrow boat, propelled by one or more people (depending on the size of canoe), using single-bladed paddles. The paddlers face in the direction of travel, in either a seated position, or kneeling on the bottom of the boat. Canoes are open on top, and pointed at both ends.","An oversize, usually older, luxury car."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To ride or paddle a canoe.","To split someone's head with a round."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/canoe"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Canoe","blocks":[{"text":"A canoe is a lightweight, narrow watercraft, typically pointed at both ends and open on top, propelled by one or more seated or kneeling paddlers facing the direction of travel and using paddles."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Morris-canoe-600.jpg/330px-Morris-canoe-600.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canoe"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Birchbark_canoe%2C_Abbe_Museum%2C_Bar_Harbor%2C_ME_IMG_2301.JPG/500px-Birchbark_canoe%2C_Abbe_Museum%2C_Bar_Harbor%2C_ME_IMG_2301.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Birchbark_canoe%2C_Abbe_Museum%2C_Bar_Harbor%2C_ME_IMG_2301.JPG/1280px-Birchbark_canoe%2C_Abbe_Museum%2C_Bar_Harbor%2C_ME_IMG_2301.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Birchbark_canoe%2C_Abbe_Museum%2C_Bar_Harbor%2C_ME_IMG_2301.JPG/500px-Birchbark_canoe%2C_Abbe_Museum%2C_Bar_Harbor%2C_ME_IMG_2301.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Birch bark canoe at Abbe Museum in Bar Harbor, Maine","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABirchbark_canoe%2C_Abbe_Museum%2C_Bar_Harbor%2C_ME_IMG_2301.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/FAHopkins_Shooting_Rapids.jpg/500px-FAHopkins_Shooting_Rapids.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/FAHopkins_Shooting_Rapids.jpg/500px-FAHopkins_Shooting_Rapids.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Frances Anne Hopkins: Shooting the Rapids (Quebec) (1879), Voyageur canoe","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFAHop